{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>If the men have come to call you</b> — If the invitation is for you, and you think to take a fee for it, arise, go with them, but perforce the word that I shall speak to you — that you shall do. And yet \"and Balaam went\" — he said: perhaps I will persuade Him and He will consent — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 22:20). \"And Elohim's anger was kindled because he was going\" (Numbers 22:22) — He saw that the matter was not good in the eyes of the Omnipresent and he yearned to go; this too is the language of the Rav.\n\nAnd Ibn Ezra wrote: The Gaon said: If a questioner will object and say, after He said to him, \"You shall not go with them\" (Numbers 22:12), how did He say to him, \"Arise, go with them\"? One may answer that HaShem did not want him to go with the first ones until princes more honored than they should come. And in my opinion this is unnecessary, for its reason is like \"Send for yourself men\" (above, 13:2), for HaShem said to Israel, \"Go up, conquer\" (Deuteronomy 1:21), but they did not believe; rather they said, \"Let us send men\" (ibid. v. 22), so Moses asked HaShem. And so here, for what need did he have to say, \"that I may know what HaShem will further speak to me\" (Numbers 22:19), except that he thought an evil thought in his heart, and HaShem said to him, \"Go with them,\" only beware not to speak anything except what I tell you. And the proof is the interpretation of \"And Elohim's anger was kindled because he was going.\" These are his words.\n\nAll of this does not seem correct to me, for what the Gaon said is not so, for HaShem said to him: \"You shall not go with them; you shall not curse the people, for they are blessed\" (Numbers 22:12) — in order that he not curse the people, He prevented him, so how would it be permitted for him to go with other princes? He did not prevent him from going because of any deficiency in the princes' stature. And what Ibn Ezra said is not correct, that Elohim would relent and reverse His decree because of the obstinacy of the questioner. And the matter of \"Send for yourself men\" was not so, and I have already explained its reason (above, 13:2). Elohim would not punish for a matter in which He grants permission — far be it.\n\nAnd in the midrash (Numbers Rabbah 20:12) they said: \"From here you learn that along the path a person wants to go, he is led.\"\n\nWhat seems correct in my eyes regarding this matter is that at first HaShem prevented him from cursing the people, for they are blessed. And why should he go with them if he will not curse them and they will have no desire for him for any other purpose? Therefore He said: do not go with them, do not curse the people, for they are blessed. Now it is known that Balaam reported to them the words of Elohim, and Balak sent to him a second time, for he did not believe, and he added honor to him with princes many and more honored than the first ones, and he promised to multiply his fee and honor. And Balaam answered them that the matter depends neither on wealth nor on his will, but all is in the hand of HaShem, and he would ask Him further what He would command him. And he did what was fitting, for what did he know of the knowledge of the Most High? And the counsel of HaShem for the world is good, and He guides those who stray on the way, and He would inform him what he should answer the messengers of the nations, or tell him what would happen to them in the future.\n\nNow HaShem said to him: I have already made known to you that the people are blessed and you cannot curse them. And now they have returned before you, and if to call you they have come, meaning that they would be satisfied with your going with them on the condition that you not curse the people, as I made known to you from the start — \"Arise, go with them, but the word that I shall speak to you — that you shall do,\" so that even if I command you to bless — you shall bless them and not fear Balak. This is the meaning of \"if to call you.\"\n\nAnd so it was the desire of the honored HaShem from the beginning, that he go with them after He informed them that he would not curse them, and that he conduct himself regarding their matter as He would command, for it is desirable before Him, blessed be He, that he bless Israel from the mouth of a prophet to the nations. Now it was incumbent upon Balaam to tell this to the princes of Balak and to say: Behold, HaShem has permitted me to be called by you only, but on the condition that I not curse the people, and on the condition that if He commands me to bless, I will bless them. And if they would not be satisfied with this, they would leave him, for even in this second time Balak said, \"come now, curse for me this people\" (Numbers 22:17) — he has no desire for him to inform him of the future nor for any other matter except to curse the people. Now Balaam, out of the abundance of his desire to go, did not inform them of this and said nothing to them, and he arose in the morning and saddled his donkey and went with them, as one who wishes to fulfill their desire. Therefore the anger of HaShem was kindled because he was going, for had he informed them he would not have gone.\n\nAnd furthermore, there was a profanation of HaShem's name in this, for since he went with them without explanation while he was under HaShem's permission, they thought that He had given him permission to curse the people for them, and that He had reversed what He had first said, \"You shall not curse the people, for they are blessed,\" contrary to what he had told them. And when they would see further that he would not curse them, they would say that afterward He reconsidered, or that He mocked them as one mocks a man. Far be it from HaShem to do such a thing, for the Glory of Israel does not relent:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The lemma from 1 Samuel 15:29 (נֵצַח יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא יִשְּׁחֵט ולא ינחם) is rendered with 'Glory' for נֵצַח following the rabbinic equivalent הדר / תפארת; Ramban's text reads לא ינחם (relent) rather than the biblical לא ישחט (lie / change His mind)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:12.029277270Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}